# Authentication

Authentication is optional. Requests without an API key are still supported and
return the same data as requests with one.

API key header validation is currently disabled to give providers and third-party
services time to adapt their requests. While disabled, `Authorization` headers
are not enforced and key usage is not recorded. A request whose `Authorization`
header is not an active API key is still served, but the response includes:

- an `API-Auth-Notice` header explaining that such requests will be rejected
  with `401 Unauthorized` in future
- a `Link` header with `rel="deprecation"` pointing to this page

If you see these headers, remove the `Authorization` header from your requests or
send an active API key. Requests without an `Authorization` header, or with an
active key, receive no notice. The header validation behaviour described below
applies when it is enabled. Keys in URLs are always rejected.

## Getting an API key

To get an API key, [contact us](./support.html) with:

- the name of the organisation or integration that will use the key
- the email address of the person or team responsible for it

A DfE support user creates the key through "API keys" in the support interface
and shares it with you securely. We can create up to 5 active keys for each
email address.

The complete key is shown only once, when it is created, so save it somewhere
secure. We cannot show it again or recover it. If a key is lost or exposed,
contact us to revoke it and we will create a new one.

## Making a request with a key

Send the key in the `Authorization` header using the `Bearer` scheme:

```
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY" \
  https://api.publish-teacher-training-courses.service.gov.uk/api/public/v1/subjects
```

Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your key.

Never put a key in the URL, either in the path or as a query parameter. Any
request with an API key in its URL is rejected, even if it also sends a valid
`Authorization` header.

## 401 Unauthorized responses

When header validation is enabled, an `Authorization` header must contain an
active key. A request with a header that is not accepted is rejected, not served
anonymously.

The API returns `401 Unauthorized` with a `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer` header
when header validation is enabled and:

- the key is not recognised
- the key has been revoked
- the `Authorization` header is empty
- the `Authorization` header is malformed, for example it uses a scheme other
  than `Bearer`, has no key, or has more than one value after the scheme

An API key appearing anywhere in the URL always results in this response, even
when header validation is disabled.

The response body is the same in every case:

```json
{
  "errors": [
    {
      "status": 401,
      "title": "UNAUTHORIZED",
      "detail": "The API key was not accepted. Send an active key in the Authorization header using the Bearer scheme, and never in the URL"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Usage limits

There are currently no usage limits or rate limits, for requests with or
without a key. This may change in future, so do not rely on unlimited access.

## Who a key belongs to

Each key belongs to the email address it was created for. If the user with that
email address is deleted from our service, all of their keys are deleted and
stop working immediately. Any integration using those keys will stop being able
to authenticate.

If the user's email address changes, their keys move to the new address and
keep working.
